      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}-13x.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x-13.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (6x-13) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6x-13}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}-13x{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(-\\frac{6x-13}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}-13x{)}^{3}}})|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6(-1)-13}{2\\sqrt{(3(-1{)}^{2}-13(-1){)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2\\sqrt{{16}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{128}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
