      ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-3x+61}+2{x}^{3}$ at the point where $x=4.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nLet $f(x)=\\sqrt{-3x+61}.$ We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-3x+61.$ Then $f=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{-3x+61}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{-3x+61}}+6x^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{-3(4)+61}}+6(4)^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{14}+96 \\\\  & =\\frac{1341}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
